The Problem: 
Even with heat spikes (Layer 1) and physical movement (Layer 2), there is still a slight chance of a false positive (e.g., a hot day + a deer running past the radar). Before dispatching an expensive drone, human operators usually want eyes on the target.

The Solution: We use Edge AI Computer Vision.

In production, this would be a rugged NVIDIA Jetson processing thermal/RGB video in the forest.

When the Radar detects movement, it wakes up the camera. The camera captures a frame and runs an AI model to visually verify the specific hazard (smoke, fire, or fleeing wildlife).

If the AI is confident, the chain of evidence is complete (Heat + Motion + Visual), and the drone is dispatched.

2. How will it be implemented? (Hardware & Software)
Hackathon Hardware: Your laptop's built-in webcam.
Hackathon Software: Python, OpenCV (to access the webcam), and the Google Gemini 1.5 Flash API (acting as the ultra-fast vision model).

Why Gemini instead of YOLOv8?
Training a custom YOLOv8 model for a 5-day hackathon is time-consuming and buggy. By using Google's Gemini API via AI Studio, you can pass the webcam frame directly to the LLM and ask: "Is there a fire hazard or a red cup in this image?" It works perfectly with zero training and scores massive points for using GenAI.

3. The Python Code Implementation
This script subscribes to the MQTT broker. It waits for the Radar to publish MOTION_CONFIRMED. When that happens, it snaps a photo from your webcam, sends it to Gemini AI for verification, and publishes the final HAZARD_VERIFIED payload.
